# STAGECRAFT 3D — INTERACTION, INTELLIGENT RIGGING & PROFESSIONAL WORKSPACE UPGRADE

## IMPORTANT: CONTINUE FROM THE LATEST PROJECT

You already have the latest Stagecraft 3D project and its existing source code.

This instruction is an additional development directive to the previous Stagecraft master specification.

Do not restart development. Do not rebuild the application. Do not replace existing working systems.

Inspect the latest implementation and improve the existing architecture.

The goal is to make Stagecraft feel like a sophisticated, interactive 3D animation application where beginners can intuitively manipulate characters, understand their structure, create poses, animate objects and produce complex movements.

The current interface is visually promising, but the interaction model, rig visualization, workspace controls and editing experience need significant improvement.

The following requirements are essential.

---

# 1. INTELLIGENT HOVER-TO-IDENTIFY SYSTEM

The current simplistic node visualization is not sufficient.

I want the studio to understand the actual structure of imported characters and objects.

When I move my cursor over a character, the studio should identify which particular body part, bone, mesh or structure I'm pointing at.

### Hover interaction

When the cursor moves over:

- Head.
- Neck.
- Chest.
- Spine.
- Shoulder.
- Upper arm.
- Elbow.
- Forearm.
- Wrist.
- Hand.
- Fingers.
- Hips.
- Thigh.
- Knee.
- Shin.
- Ankle.
- Foot.
- Individual meshes.
- Other object components.

The corresponding structure should become visually highlighted.

Show a subtle contextual label identifying the selected structure.

For example:
- Left Upper Arm.
- Right Forearm.
- Head.
- Left Hand.
- Right Foot.

The highlighting must follow the actual object structure.

Do not simply display arbitrary dots over a character.

### Hover visualization

Use appropriate highlighting:

- Outline highlighting.
- Soft selection colors.
- Bone highlighting.
- Joint highlighting.
- Mesh highlighting.
- Parent-child relationship visualization.

The hovered structure should be distinguishable from selected structures.

Use different visual states for:
- Normal.
- Hovered.
- Selected.
- Active editing.
- Locked.
- Disabled.

The interface should immediately communicate what the user can interact with.

### Important

Hovering must not automatically select or modify anything.

It should provide an intelligent preview of the structure before the user clicks.

Avoid performance problems from constantly rebuilding the character hierarchy or generating new geometries during pointer movement.

Use efficient raycasting, cached node information and appropriate hit-testing.

