# 2. CLICK, DOUBLE-CLICK AND ARTICULATED POSING

This is one of the most important interaction changes.

I want two distinct interaction levels.

## Normal click — standard selection

When I click a character or object:

- Select the object.
- Highlight it.
- Show the normal transform gizmo.
- Allow moving, rotating and scaling.
- Display relevant properties.

The existing transformation controls should remain available.

## Double-click — enter articulation mode

When I double-click a character or a particular body part, enter a dedicated articulated editing mode.

This mode must display the actual rig structure.

Show:

- Bones.
- Joints.
- Parent-child connections.
- Joint handles.
- Rotation controls.
- Selected body parts.
- Manipulation handles.

The character should remain connected and anatomically structured.

### The most important behavior

When I select an arm and rotate it, the arm must rotate around its proper joint.

It must not detach from the shoulder.

When I rotate the forearm, it must rotate around the elbow while remaining attached to the upper arm.

When I rotate the head, it must rotate around the neck joint.

When I rotate the hand, it must remain attached to the wrist.

The system must respect the actual parent-child hierarchy and local transformation pivots.

Do not use arbitrary world-space rotations that cause body parts to separate.

### Direct dragging

I want to interact directly with the character.

For example:

1. Double-click the character.
2. Hover over the left arm.
3. The arm becomes highlighted.
4. Click the arm or its joint.
5. Drag to rotate it naturally.
6. Release to establish the pose.

The rotation should be smooth and intuitive.

Provide appropriate rotation rings and visual handles.

Allow users to drag joints directly instead of requiring them to enter numerical rotation values.

Support rotation around local axes.

### Preserve the rig

Never break the original character hierarchy when editing a pose.

Use proper local-space transformations and parent-relative matrices.

Respect:
- Original bone hierarchy.
- Joint pivots.
- Bone lengths.
- Skinning weights.
- Parent-child relationships.
- Joint constraints.

Do not reposition skinned mesh vertices independently when the intended operation is bone rotation.

For compatible rigs, use actual skeletal animation.

For generic object hierarchies, use safe hierarchical transform manipulation.

For rigid objects without a usable hierarchy, provide clearly identified limitations.

