# 5. ADVANCED ROTATION, VIEWPORT AND CAMERA CONTROLS

The studio must provide independent control over the character and the viewport camera.

These are not the same thing.

## Character rotation

Allow the selected character to rotate independently of the viewport.

Support:
- Free rotation.
- Local rotation.
- World rotation.
- Horizontal rotation.
- Vertical rotation.
- Rotation around a selected pivot.
- Rotation around a selected joint.
- Drag-based rotation.

## Viewport camera

Allow the user to navigate around the scene independently.

Implement:

- Orbit.
- Pan.
- Zoom.
- Focus selected.
- Frame all.
- Front view.
- Back view.
- Left view.
- Right view.
- Top view.
- Bottom view.
- Perspective view.
- Orthographic view.

### Focused zoom

I do not want uncontrolled zooming.

Implement cursor-aware zoom and focus-based zoom.

When I hover over a particular body part and invoke focus, the viewport should smoothly zoom toward that location.

Provide a shortcut to frame the selected object.

The user should be able to inspect a hand, eye, face or other small detail without losing control of the camera.

Support smooth orbiting around the selected character or selected joint.

### Camera independence

Rotating the viewport camera must never accidentally rotate the character.

Manipulating the character must never accidentally move the viewport camera.

Make the active interaction mode clear.

